# --- Remote state ------------------------------------------------------------
# Brownbag topic 3: "Terraform state", part two.
#
# Out of the box, state is a terraform.tfstate file next to this one. That is
# fine for a laptop demo and wrong for everything else:
#
#   * it holds every secret this config touched, in plain text
#   * it is not shared, so your colleague's plan does not see your apply
#   * nothing stops two applies running at the same time
#
# Copy this file to backend.tf, create the bucket once, and run
# `terraform init -migrate-state`. S3 native locking (use_lockfile) replaced
# the old DynamoDB lock table, so the bucket really is the only prerequisite.
#
#   aws s3api create-bucket --bucket my-tfstate-bucket --region eu-central-1 \
#     --create-bucket-configuration LocationConstraint=eu-central-1
#   aws s3api put-bucket-versioning --bucket my-tfstate-bucket \
#     --versioning-configuration Status=Enabled
#   aws s3api put-bucket-encryption --bucket my-tfstate-bucket \
#     --server-side-encryption-configuration \
#     '{"Rules":[{"ApplyServerSideEncryptionByDefault":{"SSEAlgorithm":"aws:kms"}}]}'
#
# Versioning gives you a way back from a bad apply; encryption is what keeps
# the secrets in state from being readable by anyone with s3:GetObject.
#
# The GitHub Actions workflow in .github/workflows/terraform.yml assumes this
# is in place: CI and your laptop must share one state file or they will fight.

# terraform {
#   backend "s3" {
#     bucket       = "my-tfstate-bucket"
#     key          = "tf-brownbag/terraform.tfstate"
#     region       = "eu-central-1"
#     encrypt      = true
#     use_lockfile = true
#   }
# }
